  "abstract": "The Criminal Justice System (CJS) is a system comprising various law enforcement subsystems that have distinct yet interrelated functions in achieving the objectives of criminal law enforcement. Within this framework, the police hold a strategic position as the initial subsystem, as investigations serve as the gateway to subsequent stages of the criminal justice process. This study aims to analyze the role and authority of the police within the Criminal Justice System and to examine the need to strengthen accountability in the exercise of that authority. This study employs a normative legal research method using both a statutory approach and a conceptual approach. Legal materials were analyzed using descriptive, analytical, and prescriptive methods to examine the relationship between police authority, integration among subsystems, and the protection of rights in the criminal justice process. The research findings indicate that the police’s position as the initial subsystem has a significant impact on the overall quality of the SPP’s operations. Therefore, the conduct of inquiries, investigations, coercive measures, and the exercise of discretion must balance the effectiveness of law enforcement (crime control) with the principle of due process of law. Strengthening the police must be accompanied by improvements in professionalism, accountability, and oversight, as well as enhanced coordination among subsystems, so that law enforcement proceeds in an integrated, effective, and just manner.",
